Q: Why shouldn&#39;t I use protected visibility? A: <p>Okay, let me see if I can kind of put together everything I've picked up so far looking at the different references that I've been trying to understand:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct Bar {
public:
    int data;
};

class Foo {
public:
    void setX(const Bar &amp; newBar) {
        Bar tmp = newBar;
        if(validateX(tmp)
            x = tmp;
    }
    Bar getX() const { return x; }

private:
    Bar x;
    bool validateX(const Bar &amp; bar){ // run validation check }
};

class FooBar : public Foo {
    // Do stuff with x because you can setX and getX here but you can't directly manipulate x without validation
}
</code></pre>
<p>Is <em>this</em> the ideal state for avoiding using protected data members? Should I consider in this case whether or not making setX and getX protected would be worthwhile so that <em>only</em> subclasses are capable of setting and getting, and make that decision based on the architecture of my solution?</p>

